WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.690
- [Narrator] For many people
throughout the pandemic

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:05.100
dinner plans usually look like this.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.660
You look up your favorite local restaurant

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.160
on an app like Uber Eats or Deliveroo

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.570
if you're in the UK, place an order,

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.170
track it on the map, grab
the bag from your doorstep

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.810
and voilà, dinner delivered.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.010
But sometimes in those bags,

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.900
restaurants include notes like these

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.520
encouraging customers to order
directly from them next time.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.353
That would mean

00:00:25.353 --> 00:00:26.870
they don't have to pay delivery companies

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.950
commission fees of up to 35%.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.130
Restaurants around the world

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.390
have organized to push delivery services

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.920
to lower their fees.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.300
While recognizing these apps
have become very important.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.930
- They are massively crucial

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:44.060
but grudgingly crucial actually (chuckles)

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.200
it's hard giving away your
hard-earned cash to a platform,

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.010
but yeah they're keeping us afloat.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.650
- [Narrator] While restaurants
have been struggling

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.090
with the pandemic, delivery services like

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.950
Uber and DoorDash have boomed.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.460
By the end of 2020 food delivery spending

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.410
had more than doubled compared to 2019.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:05.840
But spending at restaurants
was down roughly 5%.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.310
- That will be the legacy of
the pandemic in this sector.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.960
Everyone had to change
their behavior overnight.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.650
- [Narrator] So are delivery apps,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.800
helping restaurants or hurting them?

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.630
The food delivery market as we know it

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.740
actually started about 20 years ago,

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.740
when Grubhub in the US
and Just Eat in Europe,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.680
entered the market.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.580
- The generation one operators,

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.861
the roots of this business model

00:01:28.861 --> 00:01:31.860
we're working with independent restaurants

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.330
and independent restaurants
that had existed for decades

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.870
prior to the platform coming along.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.530
I got my Deliveroo bag and my bike

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.660
and I'm gonna spend a couple
of hours doing some orders.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:43.860
- [Narrator] Giles Thorne researches

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.140
European internet companies for Jefferies

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.020
and to better understand
the food delivery industry

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.330
he spent some time
actually making deliveries

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.990
for Deliveroo in the UK in 2019 and 2020.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.723
- Everything changed in around 2016, 2017

00:01:57.723 --> 00:02:00.860
because the generation
two competition came in

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.400
and these were delivery led.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.860
These were platforms like
Deliveroo, like DoorDash,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:09.340
like Uber Eats that
saw the consumer value,

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.500
the utility of an online food delivery app

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.190
and that the branded
chains, the big restaurants

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.550
not the independent pizza parlor in London

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.690
but the McDonald's three doors along

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.420
would also benefit from the utility

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:22.657
of this type of platform.

00:02:22.657 --> 00:02:24.810
- [Narrator] There are a lot of companies

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:25.860
vying to take control

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.980
of the food delivery industry right now.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.830
The global market was projected to be

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:33.130
worth $285 billion in 2020.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.940
Margins are low meaning
despite high demand

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.760
delivery companies
mostly operate at a loss.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.030
- The reason why a platform

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.730
will not be making money
at this point in time

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:46.462
is essentially because they
are in a competitive market.

00:02:46.462 --> 00:02:49.060
♪ Did somebody say Just Eat ♪

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.520
They are having to spend
a large amount of money,

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:52.940
primarily through marketing

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.990
to get consumers, to download
the app and start using them.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:57.050
The platform says, right,

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:58.790
I got three other guys in my market

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.940
by the of next year I wanna
get two of them to exit.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.310
So I'm gonna spend a
hundred million dollars

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:05.920
buying up every billboard.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.820
And I'm gonna send a
message to the other guys

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.840
that they are not gonna get traffic.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:10.910
And that's the logic.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.200
That's why people don't make money.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.180
- [Narrator] Since the
delivery market's inception

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.330
there has been a lot of consolidation.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.710
Just Eat has undergone six
major mergers and acquisitions

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.280
in the last five years.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.870
Last year, it was acquired by Takeaway.com

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.560
which then acquired
Grubhub for $7.3 billion

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.090
in the US Uber Eats acquired Postmates

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.563
for 2.65 billion in stock.

00:03:33.563 --> 00:03:35.190
(bell ringing)

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:38.250
In December, 2020 DoorDash went public.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.440
By the end of the year

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.773
the company was worth
at least $50 billion.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.620
Uber, DoorDash and Grubhub
now control roughly 98%

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.020
of the overall US food delivery market

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.770
in terms of transactions.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:54.370
In the United Kingdom, Uber
Eats, Just Eat and Deliveroo

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.963
are among the most popular
food delivery apps.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.800
One restaurant working with
delivery services like these

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.750
is Tai Kitchen in London.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.780
- We set up the business
just before COVID hit.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.480
We literally were open

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.210
a week before the pandemic.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.680
- [Narrator] The owner Richard Poole

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.919
has relied on services like
Uber Eats and Deliveroo

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:15.190
to keep the restaurant alive

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.760
during various levels of
lockdown since March, 2020.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.910
- Those plans were in the pipeline

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.680
when it came to us having, eat and service

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.940
and having that extra revenue stream

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.510
but obviously that went to part.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.310
So we had to improvise.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:32.640
- [Narrator] Tai Kitchen
also offers its own service

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.200
for in-store collection.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.860
- We had to have our own delivery service

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.560
but obviously it's a lot...

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.560
The logistical nightmare of
having your own delivery service

00:04:41.634 --> 00:04:42.467
it's real.

00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:43.490
It's a real challenge.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:45.882
That's why we outsource it
to Deliveroo and Uber Eats

00:04:45.882 --> 00:04:48.280
to deliver our food.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:51.631
Another thing is Deliveroo and Uber Eats,

00:04:51.631 --> 00:04:54.280
they basically own all
the drivers (giggles)

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.670
It's very, very hard to find independent.

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.480
Well, technically they
are independent, right?

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:01.870
But it's very hard to find
someone who'd come over

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:02.703
to work with you.

00:05:02.703 --> 00:05:03.536
Do you know what I mean?

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:06.342
'Cause basically, they own the fleets.

00:05:06.342 --> 00:05:09.270
- [Narrator] Uber Eats has
nearly 5 million drivers

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.040
and delivery people globally

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.470
and DoorDash has over 1 million.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:14.970
Most apps offer restaurants,

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.190
a range of services

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.720
from just helping
facilitate online ordering

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.120
to actually delivering the food.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.870
If the app handles the delivery

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:26.860
the fees can range from 10
to 35% of the total order.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.040
- The whole balance of
deliveries and take-aways.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.320
But right now it's like 80% deliveries

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.560
like the whole 85 from our point of view.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.600
So I think let's say to
improve things would be

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.150
more people actually coming to collect.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:38.550
So you come collect an order

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:41.140
you basically gave the restaurant,

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.770
a hundred percent of the revenue.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:45.510
- [Narrator] These tensions
over commission fees aren't new.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:48.130
McDonald's franchisees
have complained publicly

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.420
that Uber Eats erodes their margins.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.470
While Uber has said it offers
some large restaurant chains

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.110
a lower service fee.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:57.960
In 2019 representatives
from the two companies

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.730
said they reached a new deal.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.440
And since the pandemic hit

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.000
various regions in the
US including New York, LA

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.210
and Philadelphia have
temporarily capped delivery fees

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:11.170
for restaurants at 10 to 15%.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.130
DoorDash has said the commission caps

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:15.500
ultimately restrict restaurant choice.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.410
Precisely when restaurants
need the full range of options

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.380
to help their businesses
survive and thrive.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:24.470
Some restaurants are even
working out of ghosts kitchens

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.280
where they cook food exclusively
for delivery services.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.700
- At Uber Eats, drivers have
switched off their hailing apps

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.730
this weekend to demand better treatment

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.050
from the food delivery service.

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.100
- [Narrator] Delivery apps
have also come under pressure

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:40.130
from gig workers, demanding
better employment protections

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.790
including better wages and sick pay.

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.030
The CEO of Uber

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:45.850
recently called for dialogue

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.020
but said its delivery app
allows workers to make money

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:50.770
in a flexible way.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:54.090
DoorDash Grubhub and Uber Eats
told the Wall Street Journal

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.297
they've provided drivers with various PPE.

00:06:57.138 --> 00:07:00.410
And Just Eat said it supports
contact free delivery

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:02.850
and has given restaurants
reduced commission fees

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.460
throughout the pandemic.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.580
As these companies have become a key part

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.060
of many people's lives,
regulators are watching

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.940
to see the impact on
competition, consumers,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.310
delivery drivers and restaurants

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:15.540
- It's very hard to see our money go

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:17.890
like that amount go to them

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:20.330
but again, credit where it's due

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.390
they are keeping us afloat.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.420
So even with the commission
rates, being that high

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.919
if it wasn't for them,
we'd actually have to shut.

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:28.280
(soft music)

